WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.958
- How are you?

00:00:00.958 --> 00:00:01.916
- I'm good.

00:00:01.916 --> 00:00:02.843
I'm so good.

00:00:02.843 --> 00:00:03.676
- It's good to see you.

00:00:03.676 --> 00:00:05.399
- You like a new mama?

00:00:05.399 --> 00:00:06.528
- I'm a new mama.

00:00:06.528 --> 00:00:07.361
Yeah.

00:00:07.361 --> 00:00:08.194
I mean, not that new.

00:00:08.194 --> 00:00:10.302
- They're still clapping.

00:00:10.302 --> 00:00:12.943
That does not usually happen.

00:00:12.943 --> 00:00:14.079
Get it.

00:00:14.079 --> 00:00:14.912
- [Mandy] Thanks guys.

00:00:14.912 --> 00:00:15.863
- That does not usually happen.

00:00:15.863 --> 00:00:18.240
That is the power of Mandy Moore.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.570
No, you're a new mama
now, so that's exciting.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:21.403
- I am, yeah.
- Are you just

00:00:21.403 --> 00:00:22.770
in heaven smelling baby?

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:27.740
- I mean, he's about to turn
one, so I'm like slowly.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:28.860
I know.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.241
There he is.
- [Kelly] Geez.

00:00:30.241 --> 00:00:31.120
(laughs)

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:32.220
- A little Gus.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.490
Yeah, he's almost one.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.970
So I'm kind of like
migrating out of babydom

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:37.140
into toddlerdom and-

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:37.973
- Yeah.

00:00:37.973 --> 00:00:39.060
- It's wild.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.374
- [Kelly] It's just gonna get more wild.

00:00:40.374 --> 00:00:41.207
(laughs)
- I know.

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:43.110
- Says the woman with a
five and seven year old.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.400
No, but you named him Gus,
but it's August, right?

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.233
- It's August, yeah.

00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:47.280
- So was there some, something

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.260
about the month of August?

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.120
Is that why you?

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.970
- August is when we found out
that we were having a boy.

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:55.310
And, um yeah, it's also the
month that my husband was born.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:56.143
So we just-

00:00:56.143 --> 00:00:57.690
And we love the name, Gus.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.523
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:58.523 --> 00:01:00.370
- Yeah, 'cause my husband's
last name is Goldsmith

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.670
and I like being an alliterative name.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:03.620
Mandy Moore.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:04.453
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- And we wanted

00:01:04.453 --> 00:01:05.370
to have a G, G.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.203
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:06.203 --> 00:01:07.036
- Kelly Clarkson. River Rose.

00:01:07.036 --> 00:01:07.869
- Right?

00:01:07.869 --> 00:01:09.270
- Yeah, my kids are like
that, I like alliteration.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.103
Yeah.
- It's fun.

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:10.936
Yeah, so is.

00:01:10.936 --> 00:01:12.970
- It's great for monogramming.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:14.960
It's also nice for that.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.690
No, but what's the hardest part you think

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:18.010
that you didn't see coming?

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.560
Like being a mama?

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:20.640
There's probably a lot.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.200
- I mean, I don't even
know where to begin,

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.033
and all of it?

00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:25.350
It's it is every cliche in the book.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.180
Right? For those parents out there

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.300
like life went from CPR
to technicolor like that.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.500
I mean it's just the most overwhelming.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.860
All consuming love.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.570
And it's funny 'cause
I've been playing a mom

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.550
on a television show for six
years and I'm like, wait,

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:40.940
can we go back to the beginning now?

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.800
Because I know what it's like to be a mom.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.350
I've been faking it.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:45.800
- I am so method right now

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.320
in it.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.210
Was that hard though to play a mom

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.140
like not being a mom?

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:54.190
'Cause I did hear Milo out
do a bit whenever he was here

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:55.100
'cause he said I had to teach her

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.350
how to do our first diaper.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:57.183
- [Mandy] Oh yeah.

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:58.016
- Like change a fight

00:01:58.016 --> 00:01:59.010
and you didn't know how to,
because you now have a kid.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:01:59.843
- I yeah.

00:01:59.843 --> 00:02:00.850
And I just, my husband and I

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.200
neither of us really had
any experience with babies.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.670
- [Kelly] Me neither, before I had one.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:08.660
- So I just remember having
to lean on Milo so hard

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.180
especially in the beginning of the show.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.210
- Which Milo doesn't have children.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.690
- He doesn't have children,
but he has nieces and nephews.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:16.160
- I have nieces and nephews

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.790
and I still had never changed
a diaper before my children.

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:21.050
- Milo's just missed.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:24.130
- That's your baby's
butt, take care your own.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.190
I was like, I didn't make anybody do mine.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:29.080
- He's showing me how to
swaddle, how to change diapers.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:29.930
- He's so good at that, okay.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.030
- He was incredible.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.370
And even this was like the first season.

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:34.203
And then I remember

00:02:34.203 --> 00:02:37.030
right before I gave birth to
Gus last year, same thing.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:39.180
It was like we were shooting
a scene where we had to like

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.630
change and I'm like, I'm
about to do this in real life.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:42.810
And I have no idea what I'm doing.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:43.643
- Oh my God.

00:02:43.643 --> 00:02:44.480
No, it's like that for everybody.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.550
I did hear that you had an
interesting first plane ride.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.860
- Yeah, so I took my first flight with him

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.300
when he was about four months old.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.720
And I remember being
terrified, like any new parent

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.200
that he was just gonna cry.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.770
And I didn't wanna upset anybody
or ruffle feathers, but no

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:00.810
we didn't have to worry about that.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.060
He was a fantastic
passenger, but he had two

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.660
gigantic blowouts,

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.360
like the pressure just
cleared all the plumbing out.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.193
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:10.193 --> 00:03:12.590
- And I thought we had
brought enough diapers

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:15.380
and enough changes of
clothes and we didn't.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:16.250
- Oh no.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:17.810
- No, I mean we barely made it

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:20.000
to where we had like a layover somewhere.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.270
And then when we got to our
layover, we had to do the

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:25.980
the frantic scramble to like
a new stand and buy the little

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.630
- And you start getting resourceful.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:28.800
This will happen, when you're like

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.620
in the car and that
happens, you're like great,

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.710
the second pair of clothing,
nobody put it back in the bag.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:33.543
And you're like, what?

00:03:33.543 --> 00:03:35.350
And then you start
pulling things in your car

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:36.970
and you're like I guess
I'll never see you again.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:39.310
And you're like, putting it on your trial.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:40.740
It's yeah. It's fun.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.100
I heard that Hillary Duff,

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:44.700
did she host like an
entertainment play date?

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.030
Like what happened with your kids?

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:47.110
What happened?

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:49.530
- Well Hillary is a, is a close friend

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:53.130
and she has a daughter who is
six weeks younger than Gus.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.480
And she's like super mom.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:55.570
She has three kids

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:58.010
but she decided to start
hosting this music class

00:03:58.010 --> 00:03:58.843
at her house.

00:03:58.843 --> 00:04:02.100
And there's lots of other
new moms in our group,

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.340
Megan trainer and Ashley Tisdale.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.180
So it kind of became like a bit of a thing

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.240
but there's lots of other
people that have lots

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.890
of other jobs in this
class, but it's so much fun.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.230
Like I think in this time
of COVID and you know

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:15.980
having a kid in the midst of a pandemic

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.630
like finding community to lean on.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:19.463
- [Kelly] 100%

00:04:19.463 --> 00:04:20.820
- And be able to like have your kids

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.140
sort of start to grow up
together, but also like.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:24.330
- And have those pods where your kids

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.230
can play with other kids.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.050
- For sure is so essential.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.680
So I'm really grateful that
Hillary has done us all this,

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:30.860
this giant favor.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:32.410
- Yeah, 'cause It's a lot,

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:33.920
to host something like that.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.280
Yeah, that's like.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.800
Especially a mom of three.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:37.910
Get it Hillary Duff.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:38.743
- I know.

00:04:38.743 --> 00:04:40.123
- She's trying to make us look bad, Mandy.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.630
- She does it all, for sure.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:44.600
- Your character on this
show has three kids.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.550
So you were also one of three, right?

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:47.690
I was too.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:48.523
Were you middle?

00:04:48.523 --> 00:04:49.356
- [Mandy] I'm Middle.

00:04:49.356 --> 00:04:50.430
- I'm last born.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:51.263
Yeah.

00:04:51.263 --> 00:04:52.350
But you were the only girl?

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:53.290
- [Kelly] I, yes.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:54.490
- So that's different.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:55.323
- Are you?

00:04:55.323 --> 00:04:56.540
- No, two girls.

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:57.373
- Oh.

00:04:57.373 --> 00:04:58.470
- But we were like seven years apart.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:04:59.340
Seven and a half years apart.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:00.360
So yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.730
But that is that hard
growing up with two boys?

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:05.210
- No, I mean, I feel like I was super

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:08.472
I had two super protective
brothers on either side.

00:05:08.472 --> 00:05:09.305
- [Kelly] My God.

00:05:09.305 --> 00:05:10.480
- Kind of like the show
in, in a weird way.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:11.750
It sort of like echoes the show, but.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:12.980
- My brother used to pin me down

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:14.860
and do that spit thing
and suck it back up on.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:16.990
We had different childhoods Mandy,

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:17.823
like wait.

00:05:17.823 --> 00:05:18.680
So that's kind of cool though.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:19.513
How the show was.

00:05:19.513 --> 00:05:22.890
- Yeah, it's kind of like
echoes of my childhood, but

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:23.950
but yeah, I get to live it out

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:26.270
as their mother and I'm the
youngest one in the cast.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:27.370
It's all very strange.

00:05:28.271 --> 00:05:29.860
- (giggles) Does, have you
ever actually come home

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.720
with the like older Rebecca step on

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:33.350
and your baby be like, who are you?

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:35.580
- No, but he's come to visit me on set.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:36.890
He has been hanging out with me

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:38.480
since he was a month old on set

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.500
and it is slightly confusing.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:41.370
Although I argue

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:43.760
I have to give him some
reason to go to therapy later.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.790
So, but yeah, he kind of like

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:48.050
I think he hears my
voice and he smells me.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:48.970
So he knows it's me.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:50.710
But like, you know, I have a wig on

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:52.900
on top of that and all this
like prosthetic makeup.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.054
Yeah, he was my little
makeup artist that day.

00:05:55.054 --> 00:05:56.530
- Look at his face.

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:58.223
He is such a beautiful bay.

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:00.370
Oh my gosh.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:01.330
- He is confused.

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:03.810
But you know, he's gotta
get used to it, it's my job.

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:05.030
- Yeah, to lean in.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:05.863
Yeah.

00:06:05.863 --> 00:06:07.130
Well, one of your movies,
"A walk to remember",

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:08.350
which is one of my sister's

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:10.510
personal favorite movies of all time.

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:11.931
It turns 20 this year.

00:06:11.931 --> 00:06:12.830
- Yeah.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.720
- Which, I'm a little upset
about that you look the same.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.260
- Your, Kelly.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:19.610
- You're you're like
some of us changed Mandy

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:21.920
and some of us just go in some cryo,

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.100
like, I don't know what happens

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.210
but you look like you looked
and like there, but it's crazy.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.043
- You're kind.

00:06:28.043 --> 00:06:30.250
- No, it's a great skincare regimen.

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:32.499
- I was a baby and that's
a nice fuzzy picture.

00:06:32.499 --> 00:06:33.332
(laughing)

00:06:33.332 --> 00:06:35.460
- Thank God for old like Polaroids.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:38.500
Yeah, no, I, I turn 17 on that movie.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.230
It is wild that it's 20 years old.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:43.240
It's wild that people still
connect with that movie.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:44.750
It still resonates clearly.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:46.660
Like, I like being a part of projects

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:47.880
that make people cry.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.210
- [Kelly] No, but you with a message.

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:50.420
- Yeah, no it's a great message.

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:51.253
- Yeah.

00:06:51.253 --> 00:06:52.281
- For sure.

00:06:52.281 --> 00:06:53.114
- It's beautiful.

00:06:53.114 --> 00:06:53.947
And 17 being a lead.

00:06:53.947 --> 00:06:55.830
I would, that is, seems
like a little nervous.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:56.840
I'd be a little nervous.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:58.580
- I didn't what I was doing,

00:06:58.580 --> 00:06:59.413
thank goodness.

00:06:59.413 --> 00:07:00.246
- [Kelly] So, that's good.

00:07:00.246 --> 00:07:03.010
- My, my co-star Shane West,
like had to introduce me

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:04.150
how to hit a mark.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:05.790
You know, there, you,
you were supposed to like

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:07.020
hit your mark to make sure

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:08.210
you're in the right camera position.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.600
I would like walk up to
my mark and look down

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.022
like a total amateur.

00:07:12.022 --> 00:07:12.855
I don't know.

00:07:12.855 --> 00:07:14.880
It was my first like onscreen kiss.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.780
I mean, it was everything.

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:17.613
- Is it? Okay.

00:07:17.613 --> 00:07:19.420
My first kiss was
technically on stage too,

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:21.110
in like a musical in like high school.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:23.413
And I was like, this is
not how I pictured it.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.340
This was everyone's watching.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:27.750
It was really uncomfortable.

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:31.180
- You get like the awkwardness
out of the way maybe.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:32.370
And then there's like less pressure

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:33.730
when it happens in real life.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:35.880
- Nah, then when it really
happened, it was gross.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:38.830
Yeah, he was like searching for something.

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:40.470
I was like, there's nothing in

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:41.700
you're not gonna find anything.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.250
It was just really uncomfortable.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:44.730
Yeah, all the first kiss is there.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:45.690
They ruin me.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:47.070
But, but people don't realize

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:48.530
if you're like an entertainment industry

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:50.760
or some form like that
could be your first kiss.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.200
And mine was on stage, it was awkward.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.550
- It was, yeah, it was definitely awkward

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:56.063
but I also really liked it.

00:07:57.539 --> 00:07:59.163
I mean, you're like getting
to kiss a handsome boy,

00:07:59.163 --> 00:08:02.010
there's no way that boy
would kiss me on real life.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:04.230
- I had known this boy
since like childhood,

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:05.574
now he's like a brother.

00:08:05.574 --> 00:08:06.910
I was like, I guess.

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:07.743
- [Mandy] Not a great choice.

00:08:07.743 --> 00:08:09.630
- Not a, yeah, uncomfortable.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:11.590
Well, now you're in the
final season of "This is us".

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:13.760
So what's going on with
your character, Rebecca.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:14.940
- Whoa, wait to bring it down.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:18.070
My, so we're tying up, you know

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:20.890
over a hundred episodes of
this show and telling the story

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:23.310
of this family and my character

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:24.280
at present day.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:29.280
Obviously I, I play this woman
from 25 to 85 in present day.

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:31.590
She's dealing with
dementia, with Alzheimer's.

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:34.010
So it's, it's quite heartbreaking

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:36.700
but I love that we're able
to have this conversation

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:40.330
and hopefully destigmatize the
conversation around Alzheimer

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:43.810
and including the family and
all of the, the decisions that

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:46.998
like everyone is a unit
collectively has to make together.

00:08:46.998 --> 00:08:49.730
But it's, I, I'm so excited that we get

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:52.990
to sort of wrap up this story
and in a really beautiful way.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:55.430
And I think people are gonna be happy

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:58.580
with us the way we stick
the landing as it were.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:08:59.740
- Yeah, is it?

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:01.700
I have a question 'cause is it hard to

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:03.410
snap out of that when you leave work

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:06.480
'cause y'all do cover so
many serious situations

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.480
and scenarios within family dynamics.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:11.550
Is that hard to like
compartmentalize when you go home?

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:12.930
- I think like for me, especially

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:14.310
in this particular situation,

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.660
like I grieve for my character.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:18.370
My character is very intentional.

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:19.770
She's very stoic.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:20.750
She has so much grace

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:23.430
the way she's sort of
handling this diagnosis.

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:27.240
And I, as Mandy have to not
let my feelings for her,

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:28.640
my, my sadness, my grief,

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:31.120
like kind of bleed into the performance.

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:34.460
So it's like I'll sometimes
leave, set and get back,

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:37.600
you know to my trailer in
my car and just like, ugh,

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:39.610
like it's a really, it's
an emotional release

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:42.280
but also like there's so much levity

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:44.660
in between takes, everybody's
so funny on the show.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:45.670
Like we do find ways

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:47.090
to sort of like crack jokes in between

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:49.363
'cause otherwise it
would be a horrible work.

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:51.710
- Everybody's just sad.

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.090
- Exactly, just crying all the time.

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:55.240
So yeah, we have to find
a way to like liven it up.

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:57.010
- Yeah, I love your cast.

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:58.220
We've had almost everybody on here,

00:09:58.220 --> 00:09:59.195
it's, we love your cast.

00:09:59.195 --> 00:10:00.028
- It's the cast remarkable

00:10:00.028 --> 00:10:02.450
group of people I feel
so lucky that I've been

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:04.270
like spent the last six
years of my life with them.

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:05.730
I love them so deeply.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:06.563
- That's awesome.

00:10:06.563 --> 00:10:07.460
- They're the best.

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:08.800
- Well, what has it meant to you

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:11.230
that y'all shown so much
diversity on the show?

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:12.470
Especially within a
family, like one family.

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:14.340
- Yeah, like the family dynamics,

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:16.010
but what a family can look like.

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:18.900
I mean, I'm so grateful again
that we've had the opportunity

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.800
that people have been
interested in the show

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:22.310
from the very beginning.

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:24.020
And have kind of come along on this

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:26.110
this journey with us
for the last six years.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:27.450
Like I think it's,

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.580
the conversations we've
been able to elicit

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:30.970
are so important.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.670
Yeah, it's been the greatest gift

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:34.820
to be a part of something
that's so cathartic

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:37.740
and emotional and also reflective of,

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:39.670
even though there's so much specificity

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:41.710
in who these people are in
these different stories,

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:44.440
like people are still able
to see themselves in it

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:46.230
and that's such a gift.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:47.160
- Yeah, that is a gift.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.430
Be a part of something that means, yeah.

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:50.400
- [Mandy] That means something to people.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:52.259
- Well, thank you so much
Mandy, we love having you on.

00:10:52.259 --> 00:10:53.480
- Thank you, is so good to see you.

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:55.470
- You can see the final
season of "This is us"

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:56.353
Tuesdays on NBC.

